  ["network.fetch"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
Performs an HTTP or HTTPS request using positional optional arguments. `method` defaults to `"GET"`. `headers` supplies request header names and values, and `body` supplies the request body as a string or a vector. Returns the integer response status, the response body as a vector, and a table with lowercase response header names. A program that expects a text body decodes it with `vector.tostring`. Response bodies are capped at 64 MiB.

**Requires pledge:** `network:http`]],
    kind = "function",
    module = "network",
    name = "network.fetch",
    origin = "lus",
    params = {
      {
        name = "url",
        type = "string",
      },
      {
        name = "method",
        optional = true,
        type = "string",
      },
      {
        name = "headers",
        optional = true,
        type = "table",
      },
      {
        name = "body",
        optional = true,
        type = "string|vector",
      },
    },
    parent = "network",
    pledges = {
      "network:http",
    },
    returns = {
      {
        name = "status",
        type = "integer",
      },
      {
        name = "body",
        type = "vector",
      },
      {
        name = "headers",
        type = "table",
      },
    },
    short_name = "fetch",
    since = "0.1.0",
    source = "stdlib/network/fetch.md",
    stability = "stable",
  },
  ["network.serve"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
Serves HTTP/1.1 on `host` and `port` and calls `handler` for every request. `serve` blocks its caller for as long as the server runs. It accepts connections in a loop, and each connection runs in a coroutine of its own that the runtime resumes whenever its socket is ready (see `coroutine.detach`), so a slow client never holds up the others. Connections stay open between requests unless the client asks to close them.

The handler receives one table per request:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `method` | The request method, such as `"GET"`. |
| `path` | The request target up to the first `?`, as the client sent it. |
| `query` | The text after the first `?`, or the empty string. |
| `headers` | A table from lowercase header names to their values. A header that repeats joins its values with `", "`. |
| `body` | The request body as a vector, which is empty for a request without a body. |

The handler returns `status, body, headers`, in the order in which `network.fetch` returns them. `status` is an integer and defaults to 200. `body` is a string or a vector and defaults to an empty body. `headers` is a table from header names to strings or integers. The server adds `Date`, `Content-Length`, and `Connection: close` when the handler does not set them. A handler may suspend: socket operations and `coroutine.yield` inside it let the other connections run.

When the handler raises an error, or returns values that make no response, the server answers 500, reports the error through `warn`, and closes the connection.

The server reads request bodies with a `Content-Length` and bodies with `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, answers `Expect: 100-continue`, and leaves the body out of the response to a `HEAD` request. The request line and the headers of a request are limited to 64 KiB and 128 header lines, and a request body to 16 MiB. A chunked body gives each chunk size as hexadecimal digits alone, and the line that starts a chunk is limited to 1 KiB. The framing of a chunked body, which is its chunk lines and its trailers, is limited to 64 KiB plus 8 bytes for every byte of the body. A request over a limit is answered with 431 or 413, and a malformed request with 400. The connection closes after each of these answers.

`network.serve(server, handler)` serves on a server that `network.tcp.bind` returned. That form lets a program bind port `0` and read the selected port with `getsockname` before it serves. A server that `serve` bound itself closes when `serve` ends, which happens when the coroutine that called it is closed or when accepting a connection fails.

Requires the `network:server` permission for the address, and `network:tcp` to bind it.

```lus nocheck
pledge("network")

network.serve("127.0.0.1", 8080, function(req)
    if req.path ~= "/" then
        return 404, "not found\n"
    end
    return 200, "hello\n", { ["Content-Type"] = "text/plain" }
end)
```

**Requires pledges:** `network:server`, `network:tcp`]],
    kind = "function",
    module = "network",
    name = "network.serve",
    origin = "lus",
    params = {
      {
        name = "host",
        type = "string",
      },
      {
        name = "port",
        type = "integer",
      },
      {
        name = "handler",
        type = "function",
      },
    },
    parent = "network",
    pledges = {
      "network:server",
      "network:tcp",
    },
    returns = {},
    short_name = "serve",
    since = "2.0.0",
    source = "stdlib/network/serve.md",
    stability = "stable",
  },
  ["network.tcp"] = {
    children = {
      "network.tcp.bind",
      "network.tcp.connect",
    },
    description = [[
The `network.tcp` sub-module.

`connect`, `accept`, `send`, and `receive` wait for the network. Inside a coroutine given to `coroutine.detach` each of them suspends the coroutine until its socket is ready, and the other detached coroutines run in the meantime. Anywhere else they block their caller and run the detached coroutines while they wait. A timeout set with `settimeout` bounds each wait in both cases.]],
    kind = "module",
    module = "network",
    name = "network.tcp",
    origin = "lus",
    parent = "network",
    short_name = "tcp",
    since = "0.1.0",
    source = "stdlib/network/tcp/_module.md",
    stability = "stable",
  },
  ["network.tcp.bind"] = {
    children = {},
    description = [[
Binds a TCP server to `host` and `port` with an optional accept `backlog`. Port `0` lets the operating system select an available port. Returns a server that can accept incoming connections and report its effective address through `getsockname`.

**Requires pledge:** `network:tcp`]],
    kind = "function",
    module = "network.tcp",
    name = "network.tcp.bind",
    origin = "lus",
    params = {
      {
        name = "host",
        type = "string",
      },
      {
        name = "port",
        type = "integer",
      },
      {
        name = "backlog",
        optional = true,
        type = "integer",
      },
    },
    parent = "network.tcp",
    pledges = {
